    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "research-and-use",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the evidence",
        "content": "An evidence-based introduction to Iran-related artificial intelligence can begin with specific research artifacts rather than national rankings. PersianMind documents the adaptation of a language model for Persian and English. Mozilla publishes Persian speech material for speech-recognition research. IDPL-PFOD provides generated images of printed Persian text for optical character recognition. Together, these examples show distinct technical problems being addressed; none alone measures the size of Iran's AI industry. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nLanguage is also an imperfect geographic boundary. A Persian dataset is relevant to readers exploring Iran, but its existence does not establish that all contributors, developers, or users are located there. This page therefore treats these resources as evidence of Iran-relevant technical capability and identifies commercial deployment as a separate research question. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "language-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language models and cultural evaluation",
        "content": "PersianMind's authors describe adapting an existing LLaMA2 model by extending its vocabulary and continuing training with Persian material. This is an example of building on an existing model rather than treating Persian support as a simple change of interface language. The paper reports bilingual capabilities, but its comparisons belong to the evaluated model and experimental setting. They should not be read as a permanent ranking of available systems. [s1]\n\nEvaluation has developed alongside model building. The paper Advancing Persian LLM Evaluation introduces PeKA and PK-BETS to examine language comprehension together with areas such as history, literature, and cultural knowledge. Its authors identify limitations in existing Persian evaluation frameworks and seek broader contextual coverage. For readers assessing an AI product, the implication is practical: fluent Persian output does not by itself demonstrate reliable knowledge of Persian cultural context. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "speech-and-vision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Speech and document recognition",
        "content": "Speech recognition turns recorded speech into text. Mozilla's Common Voice Persian release supplies read-speech recordings intended for training and evaluating such systems. Read speech is a particular collection setting, so this resource should not be presented as direct proof of accuracy on spontaneous conversations, noisy calls, or every regional accent. Its documentation includes contributor metadata, making the composition of the material part of the evidence rather than an incidental detail. [s2]\n\nOptical character recognition, or OCR, extracts text from images. The IDPL-PFOD research dataset targets printed Persian text and generates images with variations in fonts, backgrounds, blur, and distortion. It illustrates the overlap between computer vision and language technology: a system must interpret visual marks and recover the text they represent. Because these images are generated, results on the dataset require separate validation before being generalized to photographed forms or scanned archives. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "retrieval-and-enterprise",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From language capability to organizational use",
        "content": "Retrieval-augmented generation combines document retrieval with a model's answer generation. Research on Persian retrieval-augmented generation examines Persian-specific models and evaluation material drawn from general knowledge, scientific texts, and organizational reports. These different document settings matter because an approach that works on one collection may face different vocabulary and retrieval demands elsewhere. The work provides an example of research directed toward document-grounded systems; it does not establish adoption rates among Iranian enterprises. [s5]\n\nThis distinction helps connect the subject's branches. Language processing concerns the treatment of text; large language models support broader generation and comprehension tasks; speech recognition begins with audio; and document recognition begins with images. Enterprise AI concerns how such capabilities are evaluated and incorporated into an organization's work. The inspected research supports these connections without demonstrating that a particular commercial implementation has achieved its stated results. [s1][s2][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "trust-and-open-questions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What deployment evidence must establish",
        "content": "A model or dataset is only part of the evidence needed to assess an operational system. The US National Institute of Standards and Technology's AI Risk Management Framework offers a voluntary reference for incorporating trustworthiness into AI design, development, use, and evaluation. It is useful here as an international analytical framework, not as a statement of Iranian legal requirements or proof that Iranian organizations follow it. [s6]\n\nFor this overview, the strongest verified material concerns language, speech, and document research. A fuller account still needs independently documented deployments in computer vision, predictive analytics, and enterprise systems, along with dated evidence on institutions and national policy. Research releases establish important building blocks; claims about economic impact and widespread use require additional evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      }
    ],
